    14	# Design analysis for combo D8/1 + D15/2
    15	# a) Concrete behavior and trace:
    16	#    The base task learns rules from local source->target legends with a nearby
    17	#    teal stencil, then stamps that stencil onto every scattered source pixel of
    18	#    the same color. Originally those translated stencil cells were clipped back
    19	#    into the input frame. In this combo, clipping is replaced by automatic canvas
    20	#    growth on every needed side. Example: on a 6x6 input, suppose one rule has
    21	#    relative stencil cells [(-1, 0), (0, 0), (1, 1)] and another has
    22	#    [(0, -1), (0, 0), (1, 0)]. If one scattered source sits at (0, 4) and the
    23	#    other at (5, 0), the original solver would lose cells at (-1, 4), (1, 5),
    24	#    and (5, -1). The combo solver instead keeps them all, so the final canvas
    25	#    spans rows -1..6 and cols -1..5, then applies one global shift.
    26	# b) Complexity calibration:
    27	#    The local legend semantics stay unchanged, so humans still infer the task
    28	#    from repeated source-color examples. The visible novelty is that outputs can
    29	#    grow upward/downward/leftward/rightward. To prevent a degenerate dataset that
    30	#    behaves like the original task, the generator forces overflow in at least one
    31	#    vertical direction and one horizontal direction whenever possible.
    32	# c) Hidden assumptions in the original solver:
